      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Systemic infections like endometritis or retained placenta may show up as persistent choćory attempts, a downward head tilt, or neurological signs such as head press before temperature spikes. Watch closely for a dull coat, increased drinking paired with small volumes of milk, or a sudden drop in rumen motility which is often the first sign of metabolic failure post-stress. Any fever that persists beyond 48 hours requires immediate veterinary intervention even if the incision looks clean.",
      "source": "target",
